          "text": "Spain should turn expanding professional training into a systematic response covering all forms of violence against women. Article 15 requires initial and ongoing training on prevention, detection, equality, victims’ rights and needs, and preventing secondary victimisation. GREVIO recognises legislative progress and new training initiatives, but provision remains uneven across professions and regions, predominantly focused on intimate partner violence, and insufficiently evaluated.\n\nImplementation gaps undermine these advances. Training mandated by Law 10/2022 on sexual freedom had not yet been rolled out. Judicial participation has increased, but ongoing training generally remains optional. Prison staff lack specific training on violence against women, including those working with perpetrators. Despite specialist police expertise, gaps persist among officers, particularly in rural areas. Health professionals’ detection skills remain limited, especially for female genital mutilation and forced marriage. Social welfare training is brief; specialist courses are voluntary and often outside working hours.\n\nIntersectional discrimination needs explicit attention. GREVIO reports limited understanding of barriers facing migrant, refugee and Roma women, women with disabilities, and other groups. Police need practical skills for receiving their reports; judicial, healthcare and victim assistance staff also show knowledge gaps. Specialist services report shortcomings concerning sexual violence, digital violence, female genital mutilation and forced marriage, alongside insufficient attention to women facing intersecting discrimination.\n\n**Policy priorities** are to strengthen systematic initial and ongoing training across justice, policing, healthcare, social welfare and education, including professionals dealing with perpetrators. Training should centre victims’ human rights, safety, individual needs and empowerment, using victim-centred, trauma-informed approaches. Cover all Convention forms of violence, gender stereotypes and specific needs associated with intersecting discrimination. Accompany training with standardised protocols for identification, support and referral, and evaluate it regularly. Draw on women’s rights organisations and specialist service providers; their involvement is currently limited, and NGOs providing training report scarce funding.\n\n**Judicial training requires particular urgency.** GREVIO considers optional, ad hoc courses inadequate for complex issues such as trauma-related behaviour and children’s exposure to domestic violence. Ensure mandatory, in-depth training, especially for judges deciding custody and visitation. They must understand domestic violence as involving unequal power relations, not merely conflict between spouses, and account for harm to children when deciding cases or considering mediation. Address post-separation risks, sexual violence, and inappropriate reliance on the so-called parental alienation syndrome. Strengthen training for forensic experts, supervised visitation staff and family co-ordinators, whose understanding of violence and its effects on children also shows serious gaps identified by GREVIO’s evaluation.",
